Hello Felipe,

this is complicated, could I ask why you want to do that?

On 02/16/2012 05:29 PM, felipe moreno wrote:
> Yes, for example, making a recording of 1 kHz tone and measure the dB
> value with a sound-level meter (at the same distance).

RMS/POWER measurement is much more complicated than what is believed.

First of all, I would not do it by playing tones (pure sine waves),
because mics and meters are not linear at all, so it could be that right
at 1KHz it has a dip/hole in the response.

You can do it by playing Pink Noise at full scale. You can do even
better, but of course it depends on the applications.  If you are
recording music, I suggest to read this article by Bob Katz

http://www.aes.org/technical/documentDownloads.cfm?docID=65

> Then I would like to set the amplitude of the recording to the dB
> measured by the sound-level meter.

That's not a good idea, read the article above.

Best Wishes
Pietro Coretto

